<section id="about">
  <section class="about-inner section-inner">
    <h2 class="section-heading" data-aos="fade-up">About Me</h2>
    <div class="about-inner__content" data-aos="fade-up" data-aos-delay="100">
      <p>
        Most engineers describe themselves by their stack. I'd rather describe
        the problem I own: <strong>getting money from one party to another
        without anything going wrong in between</strong>. I'm a
        <strong>payment systems engineer based in Nairobi, Kenya</strong>, and
        for five years that has been the work — collections, disbursements,
        wallets, card issuing, and settlement.
      </p>
      <p>
        The hard part was never the API call. It's the retry that arrives twice,
        the provider callback that lands late and out of order, the payment that
        succeeds on their side and times out on yours. So the things I build
        around a transaction are <strong>idempotent endpoints</strong>,
        <strong>one-way state machines</strong>, <strong>exactly-once callback
        handling</strong>, and <strong>daily reconciliation</strong> against the
        provider's own record — so the ledger and the money agree at the end of
        every day.
      </p>
      <p>
        I've shipped this on <strong>M-PESA (Daraja)</strong>,
        <strong>Flutterwave</strong>, <strong>Pesapal</strong>, and
        <strong>Core Banking APIs</strong>, across
        <strong>GPAY</strong> (mobile fintech in Cameroon),
        <strong>Almaza</strong> (virtual cards on the App Store),
        <strong>Yeshara</strong> (CMA-approved real-estate tokenization), and a
        cashless matatu fare system settling to SACCOs in real time. Backend in
        <strong>Go and Node.js on PostgreSQL</strong>;
        <strong>Flutter</strong> when the money has to reach someone's phone.
      </p>
      <p>
        Active in the Nairobi developer community (JavaScript, GDG Nairobi,
        Flutter Nairobi). Available for payments work in Nairobi and remotely
        worldwide.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</section>
